<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
                             https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
        Exeris Spring Runtime — Root Reactor POM
        =========================================
        This is the top-level reactor. It orchestrates the build order and
        centralises version management via the BOM submodule.

        This project is NOT a Spring Boot starter.
        It is a host-runtime integration layer in which:
          - Spring provides application model, DI, and configuration ergonomics.
          - Exeris owns transport ingress, request lifecycle, backpressure,
            off-heap memory, provider discovery, and runtime telemetry.

        See docs/architecture/overview.md for the full architectural contract.
    -->

    <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
    <artifactId>exeris-spring-runtime-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.5.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <name>Exeris Spring Runtime :: Parent</name>
    <description>Host-runtime integration layer enabling Spring applications to run on top of the Exeris execution model.</description>

    <modules>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-bom</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-build-config</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-boot-autoconfigure</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-web</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-tx</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-data</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-actuator</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-events</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-flow</module>
        <module>exeris-spring-runtime-graph</module>
    </modules>

    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <project.reporting.outputEncoding>UTF-8</project.reporting.outputEncoding>

        <!-- Java version — aligned with the exeris-kernel GA line (kernel ADR-066, ADR-068).

             25, not 26, and this is a widening rather than a restriction: anything that ran on
             26 still runs, and JDK 25 is an LTS, which is what a brownfield Spring consumer is
             actually able to deploy. The 26 was never load-bearing — nothing here used a
             JDK-26-only API. It was inherited from a kernel that was preview-compiled, and
             preview class files are major-pinned, so the whole chain was nailed to one exact
             JDK. Kernel 0.11.0 removed that stamp; this removes the pin it forced.

             Consequence worth stating: the published jars are class-file major 69, minor 0 —
             same as the kernel's. Verified on the built output, not assumed. Raising this back
             to 26 re-imposes an exact-JDK requirement on every consumer and needs a decision
             that says why. -->
        <maven.compiler.source>25</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>25</maven.compiler.target>
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>

        <!-- Dependency versions -->
        <!-- Pinned to a released kernel (clean, not -SNAPSHOT): the datasource SPI-unwrap
             bridge requires kernel >= 0.8.1, and -SNAPSHOT references fail to resolve
             (401/absent) from GitHub Packages in CI, so this must always name a published
             release tag.

             0.11.0 is the GA line of the kernel's two-track model (kernel ADR-066) — JDK 25 LTS,
             class-file major 69, zero preview-stamped classes. The companion `0.11.0-preview`
             coordinate is NOT what this pin names and must not be substituted casually: its
             classes are major 72 and will not load on the JDK this reactor targets. See ADR-068
             for the artefact model this repository mirrors.

             Why the pin moved off 0.10.2, in the order the reasons mattered:

             1. Shutdown. 0.10.2 sequenced the PAQS drain after transport teardown, so an
                in-flight request was dropped without a response. 0.11.0 adds a distinct
                `draining` state and a three-phase stop, which is what re-enabled
                ExerisWireLevelRuntimeIntegrationTest#pureMode_shutdownDrainsInFlightRequest_...
                (6/6, stable across repeated runs; it failed 7 of 12 runs on 0.10.2).
             2. Preview. 0.10.2 was itself preview-compiled, so running against it required the
                JVM preview flag on every consumer. 0.11.0 retires that requirement — but note
                that `test.jvm.args` below still carries the flag at this commit. That is not an
                oversight and not a contradiction to resolve here: removing it changes the
                build-time contract (and the compiler release, and the CI JDK), which is a
                separate reviewable decision and lands in the follow-up baseline slice. This
                commit makes the removal *possible*; it does not make it.

             Consumer-visible behaviour that changed with this bump, none of which required a
             source change here but all of which are real:
               - The Community HTTP dispatcher's hardcoded `/secure` prefix convention is gone,
                 replaced by a declared HttpRoutePolicy (kernel ADR-061). This repository binds
                 no policy yet — ADR-063 is the slice that will — so routes under `/secure` are
                 no longer protected by the kernel. Called out in the CHANGELOG.
               - EventBus.publishAndAwait runs handlers on the calling thread, in subscription
                 order; handler durations sum rather than overlap. `publish` is unchanged.
               - A failed subsystem in a parallel boot phase throws BootstrapException rather
                 than the preview type StructuredTaskScope.FailedException. Nothing here caught
                 the preview type.
               - ImmutableStorageContext gained a sixth component without retaining the
                 five-argument constructor. Only `ImmutableStorageContext.GLOBAL` is consumed
                 here, so a recompile covers it.

             The ConfigProvider priority tie introduced by 0.10.0 (Community=0 / Enterprise=100,
             kernel #217) still stands and is still handled on our side — this repo is the only
             consumer putting a second ConfigProvider on the classpath. See
             ExerisSpringConfigProvider.priority() / systemPropertyFallback(...). -->
        <exeris.kernel.version>0.11.0</exeris.kernel.version>
        <!--
             Dual matrix per ADR-028. Each line is pinned here; the matrix-sb3 /
             matrix-sb4 profiles below select which one ${spring.boot.version} resolves
             to. Bump the line pins here, never the selector.

             The SB4 pin is 4.1.x, not the 4.0.x named in ADR-028's "Matrix at a glance"
             table: that table was written 2026-05-17 when 4.0 was current, while the
             decision text itself commits to "Spring Boot 4.x". Tracking the current 4.x
             release is what makes the matrix invariant load-bearing — pinning a line
             upstream has moved past would let the CI axis go green against a version no
             customer is on.
        -->
        <!-- 3.5.14 ships fixes for SNYK-JAVA-ORGSPRINGFRAMEWORKBOOT-16191649
             (CWE-338 Weak PRNG, CVE-2026-40975), -16198880 (CWE-377 Insecure Temp File,
             CVE-2026-40973), -16201011 (CWE-59 Symlink Attack, CVE-2026-40977),
             -16191022 + -16200231 (CWE-297 Cert Hostname Mismatch, CVE-2026-40974 / -40971).
             The same bump pulls spring-web through the BOM to a version that addresses
             SNYK-JAVA-ORGSPRINGFRAMEWORK-16109615 (CWE-459 Incomplete Cleanup, CVE-2026-22740). -->
        <spring.boot.sb3.version>3.5.14</spring.boot.sb3.version>
        <spring.boot.sb4.version>4.1.0</spring.boot.sb4.version>
        <!-- Fallback for a build that somehow activates neither matrix profile (e.g. a
             tool invoking Maven with -P!matrix-sb3). Keeps the reactor resolvable rather
             than failing with an unhelpful "version is missing" on every module. -->
        <spring.boot.version>${spring.boot.sb3.version}</spring.boot.version>
        <spring.framework.version>6.2.4</spring.framework.version>

        <!-- Build tooling -->
        <maven.compiler.plugin.version>3.14.1</maven.compiler.plugin.version>
        <maven.surefire.plugin.version>3.2.5</maven.surefire.plugin.version>
        <maven.failsafe.plugin.version>3.2.5</maven.failsafe.plugin.version>
        <maven.deploy.plugin.version>3.1.4</maven.deploy.plugin.version>

        <!-- Coverage quality gate (opt-in via -Pcoverage). 0.8.15 is the first
             JaCoCo with JDK 26 class-file support (major version 70) — see the
             budgetHQ backend parent pom, which pins it for the same reason.
             The baseline has since moved to 25 (major 69), which 0.8.15 also reads,
             so this stays put: a class-file reader that is ahead of the baseline is
             the safe direction, and a developer building on a newer JDK still needs
             it. Do not downgrade it to "match" the baseline. -->
        <jacoco.maven.plugin.version>0.8.15</jacoco.maven.plugin.version>
        <!-- Per-module LINE floor enforced by the -Pcoverage `check` goal. This is
             a RATCHET, not the target: each module overrides it in its own
             <properties> at-or-just-below current coverage so regressions fail
             while the existing baseline passes. The 1.0 GA destination is ~0.85
             per module; floors ratchet up toward it as modules approach GA.
             Modules without their own tests produce no jacoco.exec — `check`
             then no-ops silently (no skip flag needed). -->
        <jacoco.line.minimum>0.00</jacoco.line.minimum>
        <!-- Set by jacoco:prepare-agent; consumed late-bound (@{...}) by the
             surefire/failsafe argLine below. A direct ${...} reference would be
             interpolated before prepare-agent runs, so the agent would never
             attach (see budgetHQ backend pom §argLine note). -->
        <coverage.argLine/>

        <!-- Test tooling. ArchUnit 1.4.2 bundles an ASM that reads JDK 26
             class files (major version 70); 1.3.0's older ASM throws
             "Unsupported class file major version 70" and silently skips
             those classes — which would let the Wall/boundary guards pass
             without actually inspecting the bytecode. That failure mode is the
             reason to keep the reader ahead of the baseline rather than level with
             it: the guards do not fail when they cannot parse a class, they go
             quiet. Aligned with the exeris-kernel BOM (archunit 1.4.2). -->
        <archunit.version>1.4.2</archunit.version>
        <!-- Jackson annotations version required by tools.jackson.databind:3.1.1 -->
        <jackson.annotations.version>2.21</jackson.annotations.version>

        <!-- Test JVM args — aligned with kernel conventions.

             The JVM preview flag used to be here as a *runtime* need: exeris-kernel-core was
             compiled with preview features (class file minor version 65535) and the JVM refused
             to load those classes without it. Kernel 0.11.0 carries the ADR-066 substitution and
             is preview-clean, so the flag retired as that comment said it would — this reactor
             now compiles and runs with no preview anywhere.

             Do not reintroduce it to make something compile. It is whole-compilation and
             whole-JVM, so it would propagate to every consumer's entire build and pin them to
             one exact JDK. That is the failure mode ADR-066 and RFC-2026-08-08 exist to prevent;
             the preview line in ADR-068 is where preview-dependent work belongs. -->
        <test.jvm.args>-XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions -XX:+UseZGC</test.jvm.args>
    </properties>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <!-- Exeris Kernel BOM -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-kernel-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.kernel.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Spring Boot BOM — governs all Spring dependency versions.
                 IMPORTANT: spring-boot-starter-web and spring-boot-starter-webflux
                 are NEVER imported in any module. Tomcat, Jetty, Undertow, and Netty
                 are excluded from the runtime classpath.
                 Exeris owns the transport layer. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-dependencies</artifactId>
                <version>${spring.boot.version}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Internal module versions -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-spring-boot-autoconfigure</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-spring-runtime-web</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-spring-runtime-tx</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-spring-runtime-data</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-spring-runtime-actuator</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>

                <!-- Architecture guards -->
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
                    <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
                    <version>${archunit.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>

            <!--
                Jackson annotations version override.
                tools.jackson.core:jackson-databind:3.1.1 (Jackson 3.x, used by exeris-kernel-community)
                requires jackson-annotations:2.21 since that is where JsonSerializeAs was introduced
                (see Jackson BOM 3.1.1: jackson.version.annotations=2.21).
                Spring Boot BOM 3.5.13 manages jackson-annotations to 2.18.2, which predates
                JsonSerializeAs. This direct dependency management entry takes Maven precedence
                over the BOM-sourced 2.18.2.
                jackson-annotations is backward-compatible so upgrading from 2.18.2 to 2.21
                does not affect Spring Boot components.
            -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
                <artifactId>jackson-annotations</artifactId>
                <version>${jackson.annotations.version}</version>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <repositories>
        <repository>
            <id>github-exeris-kernel</id>
            <url>https://maven.pkg.github.com/exeris-systems/exeris-kernel</url>
            <releases>
                <enabled>true</enabled>
            </releases>
            <snapshots>
                <enabled>true</enabled>
            </snapshots>
        </repository>
        <repository>
            <id>github-exeris-spring-runtime</id>
            <url>https://maven.pkg.github.com/exeris-systems/exeris-spring-runtime</url>
            <releases>
                <enabled>true</enabled>
            </releases>
            <snapshots>
                <enabled>true</enabled>
            </snapshots>
        </repository>
    </repositories>

    <distributionManagement>
        <repository>
            <id>github-exeris-runtime-releases</id>
            <url>https://maven.pkg.github.com/exeris-systems/exeris-spring-runtime</url>
        </repository>
        <snapshotRepository>
            <id>github-exeris-runtime-snapshots</id>
            <url>https://maven.pkg.github.com/exeris-systems/exeris-spring-runtime</url>
        </snapshotRepository>
    </distributionManagement>

    <build>
        <pluginManagement>
            <plugins>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${maven.compiler.plugin.version}</version>
                    <!--
                        No preview flag here, deliberately. The compiler argument was removed with
                        ExerisStructuredScope, the only class in the reactor that ever used a
                        preview feature (ADR-029 obligation 2, withdrawn; RFC-2026-08-08). Its
                        absence is the enforcement: a preview feature reintroduced anywhere fails
                        the compile rather than silently stamping minor_version 65535 into a
                        shipped class and pinning every consumer's whole build to one JDK major.
                        Add it back only with a decision that says why, per kernel ADR-066.
                    -->
                    <configuration>
                        <release>${maven.compiler.release}</release>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${maven.surefire.plugin.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <argLine>@{coverage.argLine} ${test.jvm.args}</argLine>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${maven.failsafe.plugin.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <argLine>@{coverage.argLine} ${test.jvm.args}</argLine>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
                <!--
                    deployAtEnd: hold every module's upload until the whole reactor has
                    succeeded, instead of publishing each module as it passes.

                    Without this, `mvn deploy` publishes module-by-module, so a failure part
                    way through the reactor leaves the modules that already passed published
                    and the rest not. That is tolerable for snapshots (they are mutable and
                    get overwritten on the next push) but not for a release: release
                    coordinates on GitHub Packages cannot be overwritten or withdrawn, so a
                    half-published release version is unrecoverable — the only way forward is
                    to burn the number and cut the next one.

                    This is not hypothetical. Deploy run 30736662274 attempt 1 (2026-08-02)
                    failed in `-web` on a flaky test, after parent, BOM, build-config,
                    autoconfigure and actuator had already uploaded.
                -->
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-deploy-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${maven.deploy.plugin.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <deployAtEnd>true</deployAtEnd>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
            </plugins>
        </pluginManagement>
    </build>

    <profiles>
        <!-- ==========================================================================
             Spring Boot dual matrix (ADR-028 obligation 1).

             The reactor source tree is IDENTICAL across both profiles. Only the
             spring-boot-dependencies import differs — no src/sb3, no src/sb4, no
             source-level fork. That is the whole point: a support statement that
             says "SB3 and SB4" has to be one codebase, or it is two products.

             matrix-sb3 is the default (activeByDefault) so a bare `mvn install`
             behaves exactly as before this profile pair existed.

                 mvn -Pmatrix-sb3 ...   (or nothing at all)
                 mvn -Pmatrix-sb4 ...

             Note on activeByDefault: it is disabled the moment ANY other profile in
             this POM is activated on the command line — including -Pcoverage. So the
             SB3 line must be selected explicitly whenever another profile is named:
             `mvn -Pmatrix-sb3,coverage verify`, never `mvn -Pcoverage verify` alone if
             the SB3 pin matters. CI names the matrix profile explicitly for exactly
             this reason.
             ========================================================================== -->
        <profile>
            <id>matrix-sb3</id>
            <activation>
                <activeByDefault>true</activeByDefault>
            </activation>
            <properties>
                <spring.boot.version>${spring.boot.sb3.version}</spring.boot.version>
            </properties>
        </profile>

        <profile>
            <id>matrix-sb4</id>
            <properties>
                <spring.boot.version>${spring.boot.sb4.version}</spring.boot.version>
            </properties>
        </profile>

        <!-- Coverage quality gate. Opt-in (CI activates it explicitly, e.g.
             `mvn -Pcoverage verify`) so the default build stays agent-free:
             no per-class instrumentation overhead on the hot-path-adjacent
             integration tests, and the agent never perturbs timing-sensitive
             wire-level tests. Mirrors the exeris-kernel coverage profile. -->
        <profile>
            <id>coverage</id>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
                        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                        <version>${jacoco.maven.plugin.version}</version>
                        <executions>
                            <execution>
                                <id>jacoco-prepare-agent</id>
                                <goals>
                                    <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
                                </goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <propertyName>coverage.argLine</propertyName>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                            <execution>
                                <id>jacoco-report</id>
                                <phase>verify</phase>
                                <goals>
                                    <goal>report</goal>
                                </goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <formats>
                                        <format>XML</format>
                                        <format>HTML</format>
                                    </formats>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                            <execution>
                                <id>jacoco-check</id>
                                <phase>verify</phase>
                                <goals>
                                    <goal>check</goal>
                                </goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <haltOnFailure>true</haltOnFailure>
                                    <rules>
                                        <rule>
                                            <element>BUNDLE</element>
                                            <limits>
                                                <limit>
                                                    <counter>LINE</counter>
                                                    <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                                                    <minimum>${jacoco.line.minimum}</minimum>
                                                </limit>
                                            </limits>
                                        </rule>
                                    </rules>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                        </executions>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

</project>
